<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/foundational/the-cold-war">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The Cold War was a <strong>44-year confrontation between nuclear-armed blocs that never
    fought each other directly</strong> — a war fought through proxies, technology, ideology and
    fear. It organised the entire planet into alignments, funded a scientific and space
    revolution, killed millions in its proxy wars — and ended with a map-redrawing
    anticlimax almost nobody predicted.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the standoff, the logic, and the ending" widget="equationStep" config={coldCfg} />

  <h2>Themes historians follow</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Who started it:</strong> orthodox (Soviet expansionism), revisionist (US economic ambition), post-revisionist (mutual misperception, "both"). The Soviet archives' opening after 1991 reshaped the answer — a case study in history updating.</li>
    <li><strong>The Third World as chessboard:</strong> Congo, Angola, Chile, Iran, Vietnam — superpowers backed coups and dictators, and the local consequences outlived the contest by decades.</li>
    <li><strong>The near-misses:</strong> Cuban Missile Crisis (1962); the 1983 Soviet false-alarm incident (Stanislav Petrov judged the warning a glitch). Deterrence required perfect performance, forever — a luck-based system by design.</li>
    <li><strong>Its long shadow:</strong> NATO and Warsaw Pact structures, Korea's division, Taiwan's status, nuclear proliferation — today's flashpoints are largely Cold War deposits.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Gaddis, The Cold War: A New History; Westad, The Global Cold War; Zubok, A Failed Empire.</p>
</TopicPage>
